---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import CopyBlock from '../components/CopyBlock.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
import compose from '../../public/downloads/compose.yml?raw';
import '../styles/information.css';
---
<SiteLayout title="Self-hosting · You’re Flocked" description="Build and host your own You’re Flocked routing packs. Start with Docker, keep the builder private, and point the app at your own server.">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-heading"><h1>Your road data.<br /><em>Your place to host it.</em></h1><p>You don’t need a server to use You’re Flocked. But if you’d rather serve the road data yourself, everything you need is in the same repository as the app.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/downloads/compose.yml" download><Icon name="download" />Download Compose file</a><a class="text-link" href={site.hosting} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Full documentation <Icon name="external" /></a></div></header>
    <div class="hosting-overview"><div><h2>Files, not your routes.</h2><p>The tile server builds downloadable road-data packs. Your phone downloads a region, then calculates routes locally. The server doesn’t need to know where you’re starting or where you’re going.</p></div><div class="hosting-flow" aria-label="The builder produces region packs that your phone downloads"><span>Build road data</span><Icon /><span>Host region packs</span><Icon /><span>Route on your phone</span></div></div>
    <div class="document-layout">
      <aside class="contents"><h2>On this page</h2><nav aria-label="Guide sections"><a href="#before-you-start">Before you start</a><a href="#start-the-server">Start the server</a><a href="#build-packs">Build your packs</a><a href="#connect-app">Connect the app</a><a href="#other-options">Other ways to host</a></nav><a class="text-link" href={site.tiles} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Default tile server <Icon name="external" /></a></aside>
      <article class="prose guide">
        <section id="before-you-start"><h2>Start small.</h2><p>You’ll need Docker with Compose and enough space to build the regions you want. Pick a single state for your first run rather than the whole country.</p><ul><li><strong>Memory:</strong> two build threads need roughly 5 GB of RAM.</li><li><strong>Disk:</strong> a single state can take a few GB of scratch space. A full US build can peak around 250 GB and leave roughly 45 GB of finished data.</li><li><strong>Time:</strong> expect minutes for a small build and hours for a national one. These are estimates, not guarantees.</li><li><strong>Coverage:</strong> the supplied catalog covers the 50 US states and DC.</li></ul><p>The published image supports both x86-64 and ARM64. It includes the builder, public tile endpoint, and Valhalla tools.</p></section>
        <section id="start-the-server"><h2>Bring up the server.</h2><p>Save the downloaded file as <code>compose.yml</code> in an empty directory. It keeps the builder on localhost and uses separate persistent volumes for private build data and public packs.</p><details class="compose-details"><summary>View the Compose file</summary><CopyBlock text={compose.trim()} label="compose.yml" code /></details><CopyBlock text="docker compose up -d" label="Start the server" code /><div class="notice"><p><strong>Keep the builder private.</strong> Port 8642 has no login. Don’t expose it to the internet. Port 8080 is the public tile endpoint. For a remote machine, reach the builder with SSH port forwarding.</p></div><CopyBlock text="ssh -L 8642:127.0.0.1:8642 user@your-server" label="Optional SSH tunnel" code /><p>Then open <code>http://localhost:8642</code> in your browser. The public index is at <code>http://localhost:8080</code>, but it won’t have packs until a build finishes.</p></section>
        <section id="build-packs"><h2>Pick a region. Build a pack.</h2><p>In the builder, choose the states you want, save your settings, and select <strong>Build now</strong>. You can set a schedule later. Starting the container alone does not start a build.</p><p>Keep both volumes. They hold your settings, build data, and published files. A normal <code>docker compose down</code> preserves them; adding <code>-v</code> deletes them.</p><p>For public use, put the tile endpoint behind HTTPS. Keep byte-range downloads working and don’t enable compression for the binary pack files.</p></section>
        <section id="connect-app"><h2>Point the app at your server.</h2><ol><li>Open <strong>Settings → Offline Routing</strong> in the app.</li><li>Under <strong>Tile Server</strong>, enter your public HTTPS base URL.</li><li>Tap <strong>Check Server</strong> to load your catalog.</li><li>Download a region. Routes will still be calculated on your phone.</li></ol><p>Your phone needs to be able to reach that URL. A private instance over Tailscale is another option if the server is only for your own devices.</p></section>
        <section id="other-options"><h2>You don’t have to leave the builder running.</h2><p>The output is ordinary static files. Publish them to Cloudflare R2, S3, or compatible storage from the builder, or serve the generated directory with an independent web server.</p><p>To serve existing packs from the Docker image without the builder, use <code>BUILDER_ENABLED=false</code> and mount only the public site volume read-only. The full guide covers bucket credentials, schedules, environment settings, and standalone hosting.</p><a class="button secondary" href={site.hosting} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Continue with the server docs <Icon name="external" /></a></section>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
